      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We will use the identity $\\cot x = \\frac{1}{\\tan x}$. So, $y = \\tan x + \\cot x = \\tan x + \\frac{1}{\\tan x}.$ Because $\\tan x$ and $\\cot x$ have periods of $\\pi,$ the patience of $\\tan x$ plus the patience of $\\cot x$ has period of $\\pi.$\n\n$\\mathrm{(A)}\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\qquad \\mathrm{(B)}\\ \\pi\\qquad \\mathrm{(C)}\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\qquad \\mathrm{(D)}\\ 2\\pi\\qquad \\mathrm{(E)}\\ \\pi$ To determine the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to analyze the individual parts of the function and then combine their periods.\n\nFirst, let's recall the periods of the tangent and cotangent functions:\n- The period of \\(\\tan x\\) is \\(\\pi\\).\n- The period of \\(\\cot x\\) is also \\(\\pi\\).\n\nThe function \\(\\tan(x) + \\cot(x)\\) involves both \\(\\tan(x)\\) and \\(\\cot(x)\\), and their combined period will be a multiple of their individual periods. Since both \\(\\tan(x)\\) and \\(\\cot(x)\\) have a period of \\(\\pi\\), the period of the sum \\(\\tan(x) + \\cot(x)\\) will be the least common multiple (LCM) of these periods.\n\nLet's find the LCM of \\(\\pi\\) and \\(\\pi\\):\n- The prime factorization of \\(\\pi\\) is \\(\\pi\\).\n- To find the LCM, we need to consider that both periods are the same, \\(\\pi\\).\n\nSince \\(\\pi\\) is a common factor, the LCM is simply \\(\\pi\\).\n\nTherefore, the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\pi\\).\n\nThe correct option is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
